## Abstract

Autonomous web agents powered by Large Language Models (LLMs) are widely evaluated on multi-step digital navigation tasks. Existing benchmark suites (e.g., WebArena, Mind2Web, WorkArena) evaluate models holistically at the whole-task level, treating web pages as monolithic HTML documents, accessibility trees, or screenshot streams. Consequently, they leave unexamined the client-side Document Object Model (DOM) observation pipelines that filter, prune, and transform web markup before it enters the LLM context prompt. In modern web engineering, factual information is distributed across distinct technical layers: visible typography, semantic accessibility annotations (WAI-ARIA), developer custom attributes (`data-*`), and structured metadata (Schema.org JSON-LD).

In this paper, we present an empirical systems audit of the **DOM serialization gap**—the discrepancy between what exists in the client-side DOM and what actually reaches the LLM context prompt. Through source-code analysis and dynamic observation tracing of modern browser-agent infrastructure (`browser-use 0.13.10`), we prove three critical architectural bottlenecks:
1. Default DOM serializers unconditionally prune `<script>` and `<meta>` elements via hardcoded exclusion rules (`DISABLED_ELEMENTS`), preventing Schema.org JSON-LD structured data from reaching the LLM prompt buffer (**0.0% token reachability**).
2. The serializer constructs HTML element tags and attributes **only for interactive (clickable/scrollable) elements or frames** (`serializer.py:L1046–L1058`). Non-interactive elements (`<span>`, `<div>`, `<section>`) have their tags stripped entirely; parent `aria-label` and `data-*` attributes are discarded, emitting only raw child text strings (**0.0% attribute reachability**).
3. Custom developer attributes (`data-*`) are stripped by default whitelist filtering unless explicitly overridden by custom runner-level whitelists.

Through direct Chromium Chrome DevTools Protocol (CDP) accessibility tree extraction (`Accessibility.getFullAXTree`), we refute the widespread assumption that "DOM pruning is equivalent to an accessibility tree." We document a **Critical Divergence**: non-interactive ARIA labels are retained by Chromium Blink's native accessibility tree but 100% stripped by agent DOM serializers; conversely, developer `data-*` attributes are absent from accessibility trees but can be delivered to agents under extended whitelisting. By pairing Blink's unique `backendDOMNodeId`, we establish a 1-to-1 node-level correspondence rate of 79.4% (27 of 34 interactive elements) on route `#home`, identifying shadow DOM boundaries as the primary cause of alignment divergence.

Furthermore, we expose the **Action-Label Co-Location Confound**: augmenting button labels with target product titles converts hierarchical reference resolution into trivial string matching. We pre-register a hardened 4-arm evaluation blueprint equipped with deterministic state-asserted verifiers and zero-dependency statistical estimators (Cochran–Mantel–Haenszel, Newcombe hybrid score CIs). Monte Carlo power simulations (10,000 trials) prove that small-sample agent experiments ($n=20$/arm) possess $<11\%$ power to detect realistic effect sizes ($\Delta \le 0.20$), establishing a strict methodological prohibition against the Equivalence Fallacy. Finally, we report a diagnostic case study on local quantized models (`qwen3:4b`), demonstrating how cumulative history expansion causes prompt evaluation latency to breach fixed 180-second step watchdogs, and proving how cancellation telemetry safely captures in-flight timeouts without corrupting server metrics.

