## 2. Related Work & Research Gap

### 2.1 Web Agent Benchmarks & Observation Pipelines
- **WebArena [Zhou et al., 2024]:** Evaluates autonomous agents across functional web environments (e-commerce, forums, GitLab). WebArena treats HTML observation as an end-to-end black box, establishing that GPT-4 completed only 10.6%–14.4% of tasks. It evaluates holistic task success across multi-page workflows, conflating layout design, visual rendering, and markup structure.
- **Mind2Web [Deng et al., 2023]:** Evaluates action prediction offline from DOM tree snapshots across 2,350 tasks. While Mind2Web establishes the severity of the HTML token context bottleneck, it evaluates static snapshots rather than closed-loop dynamic navigation.
- **WorkArena and BrowserGym [Drouin et al., 2024]:** Introduced BrowserGym, utilizing browser **Accessibility Trees (AXTrees)** to reduce token load from 15,000+ tokens to ~400 tokens. While BrowserGym demonstrates the operational token efficiency of accessibility trees, it treats AXTree generation as an operational shortcut rather than auditing whether semantic attributes inside the tree provide additive predictive value over visible text.
- **WebAgent and HTML-T5 [Gür et al., 2024]:** Pre-trained specialized models (HTML-T5) to sub-sample and filter long HTML documents prior to action planning, proving that preprocessing the DOM directly bounds downstream agent execution.
- **AgentBench [Liu et al., 2024]:** Evaluated LLMs across eight interactive environments, confirming that long-horizon web decision-making exhibits substantial performance drops relative to static NLP reasoning.

### 2.2 Multimodal Visual Grounding vs. Text Observation
- **VisualWebArena [Koh et al., 2024]:** Extended web benchmarking to visually intensive tasks, demonstrating that text-only agents fail when critical information is encoded exclusively in pixels.
- **SeeAct [Zheng et al., 2024]:** Formulated a two-stage visual agent framework separating candidate generation on screenshots from action grounding on DOM elements, demonstrating that visual perception assists planning but suffers from fine-grained action grounding bottlenecks.
- **WebVoyager [He et al., 2024]:** Evaluated multimodal agents using Set-of-Mark visual prompts across 15 live websites, achieving 55.7% completion. However, visual screenshot prompts bypass the DOM observation pipeline entirely, precluding the study of structural markup channels.

### 2.3 The Unaddressed Systems Gap
Prior literature addresses task complexity, visual grounding, and model capabilities. **However, no prior study has systematically audited the client-side DOM serialization algorithms in open-source agent libraries to measure information loss between source HTML, live DOM trees, browser accessibility trees, and final LLM prompt buffers.**

