## 8. Discussion & Implications for Web Standards ("The Agentic Web")

1. **The Fallacy of Metadata Augmentation for AI:** Web authors cannot assume that adding Schema.org JSON-LD or custom data attributes will assist AI agents. Under standard `browser-use`, client-side DOM serializers discard these elements before the LLM receives the observation.
2. **The "ARIA Stuffing" Hazard:** Standards bodies (W3C/TPAC) have warned against developers overloading ARIA attributes specifically to optimize for AI agents. If ARIA attributes provide no generalized accuracy advantage over clear typography—and merely duplicate entity text onto buttons—stuffing ARIA tags risks polluting accessibility trees for human screen-reader users without aiding AI agents.
3. **The Necessity of Out-of-Band Machine Endpoints:** Rather than attempting to embed machine data in HTML DOM channels that are vulnerable to serialization pruning, standards organizations should prioritize dedicated machine endpoints (e.g., `llms.txt`, Model Context Protocol APIs) that bypass DOM serialization entirely.

---

## 9. Conclusion

This paper formalizes the DOM serialization gap in autonomous browser agents, establishing that default observation pipelines discard rich structured metadata (Schema.org JSON-LD and developer custom attributes) prior to model prompt construction. We have empirically documented the Critical Divergence between browser accessibility trees and DOM serializers, identified the action-label co-location confound, and proven the statistical power boundaries of small-sample web agent experiments. By providing a 100% reproducible offline verification suite, this work establishes essential systems baselines for future web agent engineering.

